================================================================================================
A Grammar of Motives (Burke 1945)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the CHAPTER (14 units). Everything registered at runtime is coarser
still: 56 book-level concepts. But claim-level material is ALREADY PRESENT in structured form
and simply has no canonical slot. Concretely, on disk right now: 101 unit-level `positions`,
94 of them dicts, of which the well-formed ones look exactly like claim records —
gm-00-deep.json positions[] carry {id: 'P-AMBIGUITY-AS-RESOURCE', claim: '<one-sentence
proposition>', book_page: 18, critique_target: '<who it argues against>', centrality_tier: 1};
48 of the 101 are page-anchored. Add 12 more book-level POSITIONs each with a `claim` field
(book-level-ontology.json:593 shows the shape). Add 70 unit-level `tensions`, each a named
contested proposition-pair (35 page-anchored), plus 16 canonical tensions and 21 unmatched raw
tensions with per-unit provenance. Add 441 concept records (290 page-anchored) with
paraphrastic definitions. Add 139 `outline` entries which are SUB-CHAPTER sections with
book_pp/pdf_pp ranges and multi-sentence summaries — i.e. one granularity level below chapter
already exists and is page-ranged. So the entry is roughly ONE level short of the target on
the structured side, not two: chapter → section (139 outline records, exists) → claim (113
position records, exists but heterogeneous) → clause (does not exist anywhere). The clause
level is entirely absent: nothing on disk carries a sentence offset, a character span, or a
bbox; the finest locator is a book page number. On the unstructured side the raw material is
dense — 59,379 words / ~2,485 sentences of unit prose plus 39,356 words of synthesis prose,
page-anchored throughout — from which claim extraction is a parsing problem, not an authoring
problem. What would have to be added: (1) a canonical claim record type with a stable ID, a
normalised page+paragraph or char-span locator, a provenance tag, and edges to the concepts it
involves; (2) normalisation of the 101 existing positions (7 are bare strings, 40 lack a
prefixed ID, 53 lack a page) into that type; (3) a compiler slot so claims and tensions reach
compiled-index.json at all — today neither does. Cost is materially lower than a rebuild
because the hard scholarly work (reading 518 pages, anchoring claims to pages) is done.

================================================================================================
A Rhetoric of Motives (Burke 1950)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the CHAPTER (8 units, RM-00..RM-07). Runtime sees only 39 book-level
concepts. But — and this is the more interesting finding — this entry's claim layer is BETTER
FORMED than the Grammar's. All 53 `positions` are dicts, 100% of them carry an `id`, 47 of 53
carry a `book_page`, and every one carries a `provenance` tag; rm-05-deep.json positions[0] =
{id:'P1', claim:'Vocabulary has three irreducible orders — positive, dialectical, ultimate —
each with distinct logical form and ontological reference; conflating them produces
terministic impropriety.', book_page:183, provenance:'burke-direct'}. That is a clean, page-
anchored, provenance-tagged claim record. There are 53 of them and they reach nothing. Below
chapter there are also 84 `outline` records (section-level, with book_pp/pdf_pp ranges) and 35
`calleja_pim_candidates` (each a typed cross-domain mapping with rationale and mandatory
provenance='anticipatory-projection'). So the entry already encodes chapter → section (84) →
claim (53), and is missing only the clause level entirely: nothing carries a sentence offset,
char span or bbox, and the finest locator anywhere is a book page number. The concept layer is
the weak link — 143 of 160 concepts have no locator at all — so a claim/clause programme would
have to backfill concept anchors before it could tie claims to the concepts they involve.
Unstructured claim material is abundant: 34,426 words / ~1,513 sentences of page-anchored unit
prose plus 14,670 words of synthesis prose. What must be added: (1) a registered claim type so
the 53 existing positions reach compiled-index.json; (2) page anchors for the 143 unanchored
concepts; (3) a sub-page (paragraph or char-span) locator, which does not exist anywhere in
this corpus; (4) a tension-edges.json in the schema the compiler actually parses. Items (1)
and (4) are cheap and would immediately triple this entry's runtime footprint.

================================================================================================
Aristotelian Emotion Secondary (1975-2015)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest unit actually encoded = the CHAPTER/SECTION, with claim-shaped objects beneath it that
lack identity. `outline` holds 44 objects keyed by page range ('Introduction (1-7)', summary
80 words); `concepts` holds 99 objects {id, label, tier, gloss}; `readings` 45 objects each a
full thesis; `aristotle_loci` 168 objects {work, bekker, role} where `role` is already a claim
('THE definition of the passions: states on account of which we change/differ pros tas
kriseis, accompanied by pain and pleasure'); `tensions` 27 objects typed against dissertation
questions ({'id':'TN1','type':'dissertation-T1-2','desc':'Dow is the ALLY: passions involve
the subject\'s affirming their content…'}). Distance to claim level is ONE level and it is an
identity problem, not a content problem: 45 readings + 27 tensions + 99 concept glosses = 171
claim-shaped objects already exist, they just collide on C1..C26 and cannot be addressed.
Distance to clause level: two levels — Bekker line-pairs are recorded on the Aristotle side
(168 loci), but no sentence or clause offset exists on the secondary side. Notably, this entry
has the FINEST-GRAINED EDGE LAYER of the four: unlike RDR2 and 7 of 8 Phantasia units, all
five AES edge files use concept-level or term-level sources (99 distinct sources in global-
edges.csv, e.g. 'affirmation as yes-saying', 'appetite-akrasia', "Odysseus's anger (thumos-
akrasia)", "'last protasis'"). The edges ARE claim-to-claim. They are unusable only because
the endpoints are free-text labels rather than IDs — which makes this the cheapest entry in
the batch to lift to claim level: assign IDs to the existing 99 sources and the graph is
already there.

================================================================================================
Aristotelian Motion and Time Secondary
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest unit actually encoded = the SECTION, and this is the only entry in the batch whose EDGE
ENDPOINTS ARE PROPERLY TYPED AND ID'D. amt-01-bowin-edges.csv carries edge_id, contributor,
source_type, source_id, relation, target_type, target_id, target_label, evidence_locus,
strength, note — 11 columns, and all 58 source_ids resolve to declared objects (C01..C21,
R01..R05, L01..L67, G01..G32, T01..T06, plus the unit). 67 aristotle_loci are recorded at
Bekker line-pair precision with per-locus roles ('Phys. 4.10.218a8-21 — Puzzle of the ceasing
instant; Bowin §3, referenced as temporal-passage metaphysics, not perception of time
passing'), which is clause-granular on the primary-text side. Distance to claim level on the
secondary side is ONE level: 21 concepts and 5 readings carry summaries that ARE claims
('Perceiving time and grasping time intellectually are distinct modes of cognition; perceiving
time does NOT presuppose grasping time intellectually'), and 6 tensions are stated as claim-
pairs — roughly 32 claim-shaped objects with STABLE IDs, which no other entry in the batch
achieves. Distance to clause level: one further level; no sentence offsets exist. The striking
finding is that the entry the brief calls 'abandoned mid-creation' is in fact the ONLY one in
the batch whose edge layer is already claim-addressable end to end. What it lacks is scale (1
unit, 58 edges) and any cluster layer — and, fatally, an ontology file. If a redesign wants a
schema exemplar, this is it: 37 of 58 edges are still UNIT-sourced, but 21 are already
CONCEPT/LOCUS/TENSION/GREEK-TERM-sourced with resolvable IDs.

================================================================================================
Aristotelian Phantasia Secondary (1985-2017)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest unit actually encoded = the SECTION and, for one unit, the CONCEPT. The unit records
reach two sub-levels below the article: `section_structure` (51 objects, each with section_id,
heading, pdf_pages, book_pages, summary) and `outline` (181 objects at paragraph-cluster
granularity, e.g. phx-04-caston.json outline[5]: 'Cluster 6 (PDF 6, book 26, §II cont.):
Caston distinguishes (1) thinking consists in the SAME KIND of process as perceiving, and
(1\') thinking does not differ from perceiving in any way at all. Theophrastus\' formula ἢ
ταὐτὸν ἢ παραπαλήσιον…'). Distance to claim level is ONE level, not two — and for the
Aristotle axis it is effectively ZERO: phx-aristotle-loci-concordance.json holds 288 canonical
Bekker loci resolved to page+column with 463 per-unit theme annotations attached, which is
clause-granular on the primary-text side. Crucially, ONE UNIT ALREADY DOES CLAIM-LEVEL EDGES:
phx-03-frede-edges.csv is the only per-unit file in the batch whose sources are concept IDs
rather than the unit — 18 distinct sources including PHX-03-FREDE:C10, :C11, :R1, :R2, :T1,
:T3. The other seven files are stars from the unit. So the format demonstrably CAN carry
claim-level structure and one unit proves it; the gap is uniformity, not capability. Already-
present unstructured claim material: 66 `readings` objects each with a full thesis statement
plus key_anchors (phx-04-caston readings[1] states the causal-powers thesis in 60 words with
three Bekker anchors and a subjunctive formalisation), 46 `tensions` each stated as a claim-
pair with Bekker citations, 235 `concepts` with glosses, and 181 outline clusters — roughly
528 claim-shaped objects already authored, plus 66k words of unit prose and 28k of synthesis
prose. Reaching clause level would additionally require sentence-offset anchoring, which
nothing in the entry has.

================================================================================================
Aristotle - Complete Works
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The finest STRUCTURED unit is the analysis unit — a Metaphysics book, a Physics book, or a De
Anima chapter-group (34 units over 553 PDF pages, i.e. ~16 pages per unit; the analyses run
1,147-5,332 words each). That is two levels above the target. BUT claim-level and clause-level
material is ALREADY AUTHORED, in bulk, inside the prose, and that changes the economics of the
whole programme. Quantified over the 34 phase2-*.md (158,205 words): 1,539 bullets, of which
973 carry an explicit Bekker locus (63% — roughly 29 addressable claims per unit); 2,323
DISTINCT Bekker loci with line numbers (e.g. `429a1–2`, `431a14–17`); 1,594 quoted strings of
25+ characters; 1,891 markdown table rows; and 129 already-IDed propositions under a `UNIT-NN-
Pn` convention (156 distinct IDs referenced) in 20 of the 34 files. Add 188 bekker-index loci
each already bound to a concept list and a unit_id, and 164 Greek lemmas with 1,000+ citation
strings. Crucially, Bekker line numbers ARE the canonical clause-level address system for
Aristotle, and this entry already records them at that resolution — so the corpus is not
missing clause-level ADDRESSES, it is missing (a) IDs on the ~844 unIDed claim bullets, (b)
any JSON serialization of any of it, (c) trustworthy page/offset anchors (see the CRITICAL
locator defects — the JSON layer's pdf_pages are wrong by up to 47 pages while the prose
layer's are verified correct), and (d) character offsets into the extracted text for span-
level retrieval. What would have to be added: a per-claim record {claim_id, unit_id,
bekker_locus, verified_pdf_page, char_span, claim_text, quoted_evidence, relation_edges[],
tension_refs[]}, minted by parsing the existing bullets. The barrier is not authorship — it is
that 34 analyses use 30+ different section-heading conventions and 6 different metadata key
spellings, so extraction is 34 bespoke parsers today. Standardising the template retroactively
is the single highest-leverage move; it converts ~973 existing claims into structure at near-
zero analytic cost. The entry is EXCELLENT by its own standard (a). It is structurally
incapable of (b) as it stands, but it is closer to (b) than any layout-family peer, because
the clause-level anchor system is already in the prose.

================================================================================================
Boredom Experiment (VR Attention Study)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the DATASET-CHANNEL, 7 of them. That is not claim-level at all — it
is a level ORTHOGONAL to claims: the entry deliberately holds measurement structure and pushes
findings to vle-02 (manifest.json:7 'the study's findings are registered as vle nodes 10-14
there and are NOT duplicated here'). Within its own scope the granularity is actually good:
manifest.json:24-32 gives each channel 6-7 typed attributes (id, modality, register, n, unit,
status, o9/o10), which is finer than anything else in the batch encodes. But it means BEX
contributes essentially ZERO claim-level material by design — bex-01's four load-bearing cases
(S03/S04/S05/S07) are the only claim-shaped objects, and they are duplicated verbatim from
vle-02:13-16. The batch's claim-level content is elsewhere. The real question this entry
raises is different: because it is a channel schema rather than an analysis, 'full treatment'
on the cluster template is the wrong target. What it needs is a DATASET record type —
channels, cohorts, gates, processing state, provenance register — which the manifest already
IS, and which the runtime cannot see. To reach claim level would require lifting bex-01's
aggregates (16/24 dilation, 6/8 depletion, boredom means 7.0/6.0/4.5) into records; but doing
that here would violate the entry's own no-duplication rule, so the right move is to make
vle-02's findings claim-level and leave BEX as the dataset descriptor it correctly is.

================================================================================================
Boredom Secondary (Part III)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The finest STRUCTURED unit is the sub-item inside a unit JSON, not the unit itself — and this
is the single most important correction to make about this entry. It encodes 2,924 sub-items
across 55 units (463 readings, 425 constructs, 582 concepts, 718 interlocutors, 387 sections,
211 tensions, 138 measures), of which 2,012 of the 2,258 dict-shaped ones carry a page locus.
The `readings` array IS claim-level already: each item is a single proposition with a page
anchor, an argumentative stance, and a provenance tag distinguishing the source's own claim
(FE) from a close paraphrase (FAITHFUL-<author>) from the project's own interpretive extension
(P/anticipatory-application). Example from bor-sec-13: {"claim": "Beings' 'telling refusal' in
profound boredom is itself 'a telling, i.e. a making manifest' — it announces the very
possibilities it withholds…", "type": "READING/CLAIM", "stance": "restates-FCM (fcm-06)",
"locus": "p.114-116", "tag": "FAITHFUL-Slaby"}. That is one level below the article and
roughly at the target. So the distance to claim-level is NOT authorship — it is schema and
addressing: (1) the readings array has 9 incompatible sub-schemas (claim/text/summary;
locus/loci/page); (2) reading ids are unqualified local counters that collide across 6 units;
(3) `stance` has 162 uncontrolled values with 195 nulls; (4) nothing reaches runtime — the
compiler reads no JSON except tension-edges.json, which this entry does not have. A
normalization pass over 55 files (unify to {id: 'bor-sec-NN-Rmm', claim, locus, stance∈enum,
tag∈enum}) would deliver ~460 addressable claims at a fraction of the cost of re-reading the
sources. Clause-level is genuinely absent: no sub-item is smaller than a proposition, and no
quoted span carries a character offset or bbox. But the raw material for it is sitting in the
prose layer — 1,725 quoted spans of ≥40 characters across the 55 .md analyses (1,026,780
bytes), roughly 31 per unit, the great majority of which sit within 200 characters of a page
locus. Those are extractable quotation records today; what they lack is a verbatim-
verification link back to the PDF text, which is exactly what the WRAITH verbatim/bbox work
exists to supply. Where the entry is structurally incapable of the target: the EDGE layer.
1,918 rows keyed on (source, relation, target) with locus as a free-text note cannot express a
claim-to-claim relation, only unit-to-string. And global-edges.csv's dedup key deletes locus
outright — 21 rows already lost. Any redesign must move edges from unit-level to claim-level
endpoints, or the finest relational data will keep being collapsed by its own synthesis step.

================================================================================================
Dissertation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This entry is the exception in the whole corpus index: it ALREADY encodes claim-level
granularity, and it does so structurally, not incidentally. 982 distinct claim records (306
for §1.4 alone) across 56,713 words of source prose = one record per ~58 words, which is
roughly sentence granularity. 100% carry a line anchor (`ln` string or `location`
{line_start,line_end}); 76% participate in an explicit supports/supported_by graph; every
record carries a typed support tier from an 8-level legend (T1 textually-confirmed 277, T2 78,
T3 interpretive-but-flagged 477, T4 41, T5 71, T6 12, T7 overreach 22, T8 secondary-needed 4);
38% record the primary citations actually present in the text; 11% record
`implicit_citations_needed` with a candidate locus, a tier, and a rationale; and nearly all
carry a `remediation` object naming an action plus corpus-index / ChromaDB / Perplexity
candidates and an effort estimate. Below claim level, the entry also holds genuinely CLAUSE-
level material: 841 typed edges whose `target` column is 53% claim-IDs and 47% free-text loci
(ARISTOTLE-LOCUS:'DA III.7 431a8-12', HEIDEGGER-LOCUS:'SZ §29 H.137', SECONDARY-LOCUS:'Burke
Grammar pp. 280-28', SECTION:DISS-02-A1A2); 235 cached verbatim quotations each with
author/year/page, an `insertion_anchor` naming the exact phrase in the target text, a use
designation (in-text/footnote/epigraph) and a rationale; and 156 citation-fill markdown files
that pair a verbatim with the exact current sentence it is to attach to, plus a ready-to-paste
MLA/LaTeX form. Spot-verified: the Christensen 2016 verbatim in cache-Q-CHR-05.md reproduces
corpus/download/_extra_Aristotle_20on_20Anger_20Justice_20and_20Punishment.pdf exactly. So the
cost of the claim/clause-level programme for this entry is NOT extraction — it is (a) schema
unification across six dialects, (b) promoting the 156 citation-fill .md files and the free-
text LOCUS strings into resolvable IDs, and (c) re-anchoring against the 3 drifted sources.
The one structural limit: all of it is about the user's OWN prose, not about a source text. To
serve the stated goal for SOURCES, this schema would have to be re-pointed at corpus documents
— but it is a working, proven, claim-level schema and the only one in the index.

================================================================================================
Game-Behavior Analysis Method (Burke × Calleja)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This entry sits at the OPPOSITE end from the batch's other member: its finest unit of analysis
is the whole method-document, and there is no unit layer at all — no per-section records, no
claim records, no edges, no ontology. Its structured content is exactly 11 crosswalk bindings
in validated-crosswalk.json (4 core bijective + 4 many-to-many cluster + 3 secondary ties, 0
rejected), each with pentad term, PIM dimension, strength, status, both_directions flag, and a
per-direction textual warrant. That is 11 records for 82,134 words — roughly 1 structured
record per 7,500 words, three to four orders of magnitude coarser than the claim/clause
target. BUT the entry is dense with material that is ALREADY claim-level and merely
unstructured. Quantifying what a extraction pass would find: 68 quoted strings of 25+
characters in CANONICAL-METHODOLOGY-v9.md alone, 24 distinct Bekker loci in that one file, and
a fully-enumerated diagnostic vocabulary that is effectively a claim schema in disguise — R1
assimilation, R2 embodiment, R3 simultaneity, R4 cornerstone, R5 blending, R6 temporal-apex,
R7 intrusion-integration appear 68/35/40/26/34/42/78 times respectively across the markdown
(323 coded assertions), each attached in the VALIDATION files to specific timestamps in one
game episode. THE-MATRIX.md is literally a claim-extraction form: chain locus x what-happens x
three-factor-motion x designed-channel x doxa/hexis/affect, with an R1-R7 criterion table
underneath. So the raw material for a claim-level encoding of THIS method exists and is well-
disciplined; what is missing is (a) any ID scheme for the propositions, (b) any file that
holds a filled-in MATRIX (the worksheet in the index is BLANK — every data cell is empty), and
(c) any edge table linking the 11 crosswalk bindings, the 7 R-criteria, the A0-A4 chain nodes,
and the sibling entries' units. Critically, this entry is not a source-text index at all — it
is the INSTRUMENT that would be applied to source texts. Judged by (a) its own standard, it is
a well-versioned, empirically-checked, cross-validated methodology with an honest changelog
and mostly-verified quotations. Judged by (b) the claim/clause-level target, it is
structurally incapable in its current form: there is nothing to encode claims ABOUT, because
the entry indexes no document. Its correct role in the redesign is as the SCHEMA SOURCE — the
R1-R7 criteria, the A0-A4 chain loci, the 6x6 Burke×Calleja grid and the four-cause/seven-
cause frame are exactly the controlled vocabulary a claim-level game-analysis encoding would
need — not as an entry to be given unit records of its own.

================================================================================================
Heidegger - Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The FINEST unit ENCODED in a structured slot is the section (§1-§28, 28 records with heading,
page and pdf_page ranges, tags, greek_terms_expected, and a genuinely fine subsection list —
u-fp3c.json alone declares §17a, §17b, §17b-α, §17b-β, §17b-γ, §18a-§18d with full Greek
headings). That subsection layer is one level finer than BT's and is the closest thing in the
batch to sub-chapter granularity. Claim-level material is ALREADY AUTHORED in bulk: 150 claim
records (U-…-Pn) each with a full claim sentence, enumerated premises with an explicit '→
**Conclusion**' step, an 'Aristotelian texts' locus field (e.g. 'Met. Gamma 2, 1004 b 17--26;
cf. Plato, Sophist 253 b 10'), and a Status field. On top of that: 189 typed per-unit edges
with prose labels, ~40 per-unit tension records, 488 inline page-cited quotations, and a
67-lemma Greek appendix with per-unit translation_status (reinterpreted /
partially_reinterpreted / standard) — which is itself a claim-level assertion about each term
in each unit, already machine-readable. Clause-level is absent: no clause is addressed, no
quotation carries a character span, and the Bekker/page loci are strings not structured
citations. The conversion cost is HIGHER than BT's despite more material, because BCAP's
markdown is irregular: two claim formats (### headings vs Field/Value tables), three concept
naming systems, 79 uncontrolled relation values, one syntactically invalid JSON fence, and 5
of 13 units with no edge block at all. Normalisation, not authoring, is the bottleneck.

================================================================================================
Heidegger - Being and Time
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The FINEST unit the entry ENCODES in a structured slot is the section (§1-§83, 83 records in
bt-structured with heading, H./eng/pdf page ranges, tags, german_terms_expected). Everything
above section level is well covered; nothing below it is encoded in any machine-readable slot.
BUT claim-level material is ALREADY AUTHORED and merely trapped in prose: 108 claim records
exist as markdown table rows with a five-field schema (`| id | claim | premises | targets |
status |`, ids BT-D1-U2-P1 style, status drawn from a 7-value vocabulary), each averaging one
full claim sentence plus 3-6 enumerated premises plus an explicit list of what the claim
argues against. On top of that, 299 typed edge objects sit in fenced JSON, ~86 tension items
carry [INTERP-low/high] confidence tags AND explicit `Linked to: BT-D1-U2-P2` claim-level
back-references (84 such links), and ~360 German-term table rows carry per-term h_pages. So
claim-level is roughly 90% of the way there in content and 0% of the way there in form — the
conversion is a parse job, not an analysis job. Clause-level is entirely absent: no sentence
or clause is ever addressed, no quotation carries a span, and 1,230 quoted strings sit inline
with only 37 locally cited. To reach the stated target one would need (a) a JSON schema for
the existing 108 claims + 84 claim-to-tension links, (b) a verbatim layer anchoring each of
the 1,230 quotations to H-page and character span, and (c) a clause decomposition of the
premises fields, which are already semi-decomposed as numbered '(1)…(2)…(3)' lists and could
be split mechanically.

================================================================================================
Heidegger - The Fundamental Concepts of Metaphysics
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The finest unit the entry FORMALLY encodes is the chapter/section unit (fcm-00..fcm-14, one
per FCM chapter or section group). But claim-level material is already present in bulk,
unstructured or semi-structured, and this materially changes the cost of the programme.
Quantified: (a) 182 records in 'claims' arrays across 13 unit JSONs, each with id + a one-
sentence proposition + a triple locus - e.g. fcm-05 C2 'Depth, not objective span of time,
decides the extent of boredom; one can be more bored in five minutes of company than in four
hours at the station.' with locus '(p.108 / GA pp.163-64 / pdf 66)'. That is already claim-
level with page-precise anchoring. (b) 63 further records in 'objects' arrays in fcm-07/08/09.
(c) The units' 'sections' arrays go one level finer still - subsection records like §25b
'Obstructive casualness (Lassigkeit) as the deepening way we are left empty' with book_p 116,
giving a section-to-subsection tree beneath each unit. (d) 1,083 inline triple-loci across the
26 markdown files and 2,090 quoted spans in the unit prose - each locus marks a specific
quotation or claim, so the prose already carries clause-anchored evidence, just as text rather
than records. So roughly 245 structured claim records + ~1,100 locus-anchored prose claims are
ALREADY authored. What is missing is (i) stable claim IDs that edges can point at - today
edges point at free-text concept names, never at C1/C2; (ii) a uniform claims key (fcm-08/09
use 'objects'); (iii) any clause-level decomposition (no entry file segments a claim into
subject/predicate/qualifier or marks the specific clause a locus certifies); and (iv) any path
from claim records to runtime. To reach claim-level the honest work is normalization and
wiring, not authoring. To reach clause-level, genuinely new work is required across all 15
units.

================================================================================================
Heidegger and Rhetoric
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The finest formally-encoded unit is the essay (or half-essay: Gross is split A/B at book
p.27). That is one level coarser than a chapter in FCM terms. But substantial claim-level
material already exists inside the phase-2 records: 74 'positions' across the 8 units (each an
essay-level proposition with an id such as HR-01-GROSS-A-P2 and a page anchor), 146
key_concepts, 69 tensions/open questions, 233 local edges, and - unusually - two loci
concordances that are already sub-claim-level: hr-heidegger-loci-concordance.json holds 162
distinct Heidegger loci and hr-aristotle-loci-concordance.json holds 66 Aristotle loci, each
with the citing contributor, the essay page and a context field. Several of those context
fields are already clause-anchored: e.g. the GA 18 262 entry reproduces the full English and
German block quotation with the note 'This is the volume's most-cited single GA 18 quote'. The
eight phase-2 markdown analyses (~44,000 words) and the 7 contested-node dossiers plus 11
disagreement dossiers carry a further dense layer of claim-level argument in prose, each keyed
to a contributor and a page. So the claim layer is roughly half-built and, unlike FCM, it is
already ID-bearing (POSITION:HR-NN-AUTHOR-Pn). Three things block it: (1) 14 of those position
ids were never resolved (TBD/-Pn/-P[...]); (2) positions live only in phase-2 JSON and never
appear in the ontology, the matrix or compiled-index; (3) the POSITION ids and the CONCEPT
labels use different, non-joining vocabularies. Clause-level is entirely absent - nothing in
the entry decomposes a position into its constituent clauses or marks which clause a given
GA-18 locus certifies. Cost implication: getting HR to claim level is mostly ID hygiene and
wiring; getting it to clause level is new authoring across 8 essays and ~230 loci.

================================================================================================
In-Game (Calleja 2011)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit actually encoded = a named concept or position within a chapter, with a
book-page (sometimes page-range) anchor. That is one level below chapter, not two: a page is
not a claim and certainly not a clause. HOWEVER — claim-level material is already present in
quantity, just unstructured or unreachable. (1) 71 unit-level `positions` across the 13 deep
JSONs are already true claim records (e.g. ig-10-deep.json positions[0] = {id:'P01',
label:'Incorporation displaces immersion+presence as primary theoretical frame',
anchor:'pp.167-169 + 172', summary:'…'}) — they need only a stable ID scheme and a canonical
slot. (2) 45 unit tensions + 12 volume tensions are claim-vs-claim oppositions with page
anchors. (3) 18 canonical POSITIONs in book-level-ontology.json carry `claim` +
`supporting_units` and are the closest thing to a claim ontology in the whole corpus — and
they reach nothing. (4) 995 line-range anchors are already sub-page, i.e. clause-adjacent —
this is the single largest latent asset in the entry, and it is worthless only because
/tmp/calleja-fulltext.txt was deleted; regenerating that file with a recorded, deterministic
extraction command would resurrect ~1,000 clause-level pointers in one step. (5) 47,443 words
of deep prose analysis (13 *-deep.md) contain hundreds of further claim-level assertions with
inline page cites, entirely unstructured. What is structurally MISSING and cannot be
recovered: verbatim clause text. The entry deliberately suppresses it —
ig-10-deep.json:434-441 `content_filter_audit` records that every ≥25-word block was
paraphrased, and ig-00-deep.json:222 / ig-02-deep.json:330 assert 'no ≥25-word verbatim from
Calleja'. A clause-level index needs the clause. So: claim-level is ~70% of the way there and
cheap (promote positions/tensions, give them IDs, register them); clause-level requires re-
extracting from the PDF under a policy change, and the line-anchor scaffolding to hang it on
must be rebuilt first.

================================================================================================
Presence Theory (Foundations)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the cluster concept node — 10 of them across 4 sources, one ~45-word
definition each. That is source-level, i.e. two to three levels above claim/clause. The unit
.md files sit one notch finer (4 'Key constructs' + 2-3 numbered verbatim loci per source) but
are prose. Crucially, claim-level material with explicit epistemic status ALREADY EXISTS here
in unstructured form and is unusually well-formed: bridge-to-part-ii.md §3 is a 6-row
crosswalk where each row already carries {Lepecq claim, Uexküll anchor, Part II vocabulary,
mapping status}, and §7 is an 8-row ledger where each row already carries {claim, layer,
status}. That is 14 rows that are, structurally, claim records — they need a schema, not
authorship. Add the entry's 8 drafting loci and 15 unit-declared constructs and roughly 37
claim-sized assertions are already written down. Across the entry: 69 quoted strings ≥25 chars
and 18 markdown table rows in 6,171 words. What would have to be added to reach the target:
(a) stable claim IDs (the entry has NO IDs at any level below ptf-NN — nodes are ordinals,
loci are [L1]/[L2] scoped per-file and colliding across files); (b) a verified/unverified
field, since the entry already tracks that distinction in prose and gets it wrong once (the
three Lepecq bridge quotes); (c) claim→quote→locator links, which citations.md already
supplies at source granularity via verified pagination offsets.

================================================================================================
Red Dead Redemption 2 (Salvo Playthrough)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest unit ACTUALLY ENCODED is not the clip - it is the frame. The 68 raw-frame-logs address
1,040 distinct frames by id (e_0001, l_0385, q_101, ...) with 529 t=/t~ timestamps and 1,326
bullet-level observations, at 24 fps for 02:29-03:45. That is one to two levels BELOW the
user's clause-level target on the temporal axis. Above it sit 284 timestamped beat rows in 22
beat-maps, then 32 element rows, then the prose syntheses. So the claim/clause-level material
is not missing - it is already written, at roughly 69,080 words of it, and it is 100%
unstructured: zero IDs, 24 header dialects, evidence-grade encoded in free text inside column
headers ('inferred', 'NO dialogue anchor', '[UNCERTAIN]'), and the whole raw tier orphaned
from the manifest. What would have to be ADDED is therefore not observation but a schema: a
stable id per beat and per frame-observation, an explicit evidence-grade enum (frame-observed
/ subtitle-read / transcript-derived / role-inferred / author-confirmed / refuted), a
supersedes/superseded-by edge so frame-read-verification.md's 10 verdicts attach to the 284
beat rows they judge, and a claim->quotation->locator triple so 'Oh, you struggled...'
resolves to a frame id rather than a stitched paraphrase. Cost is extraction and
normalisation, not new analysis - which materially lowers the price of the whole programme for
this entry. The one thing genuinely absent is a claim-level decomposition of the entry's OWN
argumentative prose (rdr2-tutorial-RODA.md's 5,135 words of sections 0-8 contain perhaps
80-120 discrete assertions with zero addressing).

================================================================================================
Red Dead Redemption 2 Secondary (2019-2023)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest unit actually encoded = the ARTICLE, with a per-article inventory beneath it. There is
no ID for anything below the article: concepts, readings, game_elements, themes, interlocutors
and tensions are all label-only objects or bare strings. The edge layer is strictly
article→label (all 12 files are stars from the unit id), so no claim participates in a
relation. Distance to claim level: two full levels — you would need (1) stable IDs for each of
the 1,043 sub-unit objects, and (2) edges whose SOURCE is one of those IDs rather than the
unit. Distance to clause level: three levels — there is no locus anchoring below the section
(`readings[].locus` is 'General Introduction (pp.6-7); Part 1 sec.1 (p.8)', i.e. section+page,
never a line or sentence). CRITICALLY, claim-level material IS already present but
unstructured: the 86 `readings` objects each carry a one-sentence claim plus a page locus, the
56 `tensions` are stated as claim-pairs ('Procedural rhetoric vs. player freedom: the honor
system is framed as valorizing the possibility of choice, yet Bagnoli concedes that free will
is itself biased...'), and the 38 `debate_axis_positions` are per-unit stances. That is
roughly 180 claim-shaped objects already authored across 12 units, plus 31,501 words of unit
prose containing many more. Promoting the existing readings/tensions/debate_axis_positions to
first-class ID'd claim nodes is a re-keying job, not a re-reading job — which materially
lowers the cost of the programme for this entry.

================================================================================================
Rhetoric of Interactivity and Virtual Reality (Part III)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the cluster concept node, 7 across 2 sources, one ~35-word definition
each. That is source-level and, at 2 sources, barely above entry-level. The prose layer holds
8 unit-declared constructs with centrality+register tags and 6 verified verbatims with loci —
roughly 14 claim-sized assertions in 2,832 words (40 quoted strings ≥25 chars). The entry is
unusual in that its ANALYTICAL claims are finer-grained than its structural ones: cluster-
ontology.md:13-23 argues a two-lever decomposition (Golliher = doing lever, Ulrich = moving
lever) and riv-01:44-45 states an operational TEST ('a design is "not fully interactive" when
it "does not demand that visitors" act') that is directly checkable against the VLE corpus's
A₃→A₄ stall. That test is exactly a claim-level object — it has a source, a verbatim, a locus,
and a target it can be run against — and it is encoded as a sentence inside a §5 heading. What
would have to be added: (a) IDs at node and locus level (none exist; [L1] already collides
across two files); (b) a claim record with {text, source, locus, verification status, target};
(c) a real edge to vle-01/vle-05, which the entry asserts in prose three times and encodes
zero times. Being only 2 sources and 7 nodes, this entry is the clearest case in the batch
where the current unit of analysis is simply too coarse to be worth a separate entry at all.

================================================================================================
Rickert - Ambient Rhetoric
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest STRUCTURED unit: a chapter section (65 sections, each a heading + page range + tag
list) — nominally the finest structural granularity in the batch, but each section record
carries zero analytic content, so the finest CONTENTFUL structured unit is the chapter. Claim-
level material exists but is markedly less structured than in Wendt or Uexküll: '## 4. Main
Positions' is PROSE with bolded or ### position headers, yielding 75 identifiable discrete
positions across the 10 files (ch1 8, ch2 6, ch3 8, ch4 5, ch5 10, ch6 8, ch7 7, ch8 7,
conclusion 8, intro 8) and zero table rows anywhere — versus Uexküll's 107 typed claim rows
and Wendt's 139 JSON claim objects. '## 5. Tensions and Open Questions' contributes a further
51 discrete tensions (5 per chapter, 6 in the conclusion). The genuinely machine-readable
fine-grained layer is the 242 local edges in fenced JSON, but 8 schemas and 189 relation
values make them unusable without normalisation, and 120 of them carry no locus field at all.
Concept tables give 146 rows. Distance to claim level: TWO steps here, not one — extraction
from prose (harder than parsing a table) plus schema unification across 10 divergent file
shapes. Distance to clause level: unreached; no spans, no offsets, and the page loci that do
exist inherit the wrong pdf offset. Cost implication: Rickert is the most expensive of the
three to lift to claim level despite having the most words (43,644).

================================================================================================
Social Presence in Virtual Worlds (Part III)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = the cluster concept node, 10 across 3 sources, one ~40-word
definition each. That is source-level. The prose layer sits finer but is unaddressable: 15
unit-declared 'Key constructs' with centrality+register tags, 8 verified verbatims with page
or PDF-page loci, and — the interesting part — a 4-item numbered 'Suggested disposition'
(drafting-loci.md:31-40) in which each item is already a claim about how a specific source
bears on a specific sentence of the dissertation ('1. Keep the "social interior" phrasing —
svw-01 licenses it ontologically; svw-02 instances it empirically'). That is claim-level
reasoning already written, keyed to a quoted target passage reproduced verbatim at drafting-
loci.md:7-10. Across the entry: 86 quoted strings ≥25 chars in 4,936 words — the highest
quotation density per word in the batch. So roughly 27 claim-sized assertions (15 constructs +
8 loci + 4 dispositions) are authored and none is addressable. To reach the target: (a) node
and claim IDs (there are none at any level below svw-NN); (b) a claim→source-passage link,
which drafting-loci already does in prose; (c) typed edges, since the entry's central
intellectual move is a disagreement (svw-03 against the draft's multiplayer equation) that
currently exists as a DEBATE-AXIS node with no edge and no tension-edges.json.

================================================================================================
Uncomfortable Situations
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = a named concept or position within a chapter, anchored to a PDF page
(sometimes a page range or a note number such as 'n.29'). That is roughly one level below
chapter — a page, not a claim, and not a clause. But claim-level material is already dense
and, unusually, already well-formed. (1) The 55 unit-level `positions` are textbook claim
records: us-03-radcliffe.json positions[0] = {position_id:'P-USR-01', claimant:'Gross',
claim:'J. J. Gibson's affordance theory can and must be extended into a rhetorical-historical-
cultural theory of negative/hostile affordances, against the prevailing orthodox ecological-
psychology reading…', support:'Cop-car-roof case (PDF 104-105); Aristotle's Cydias on Samos
(PDF 102-103); Lewin's Aufforderungscharakter genealogy (PDF 104-105); legal hostile-work-
environment case (PDF 108)', contested_by:'Orthodox Gibsonian ecological psychology;
Scarantino's narrower taxonomy (PDF 109)'} — claimant, claim, evidence loci and opposition,
all in one record. (2) The 18 canonical positions add `canonical_statement` + `support_units`.
(3) The 45 unit tensions and 15 canonical tensions are claim-vs-claim pairs with per-pole
statements. (4) The four loci concordances hold 176 records that are ALREADY at or below
clause level: us-03's radcliffe_loci include {locus:'Radcliffe Romance of the Forest, p.7',
context:"'Be comforted' — paradiastole/perlocutionary ironic frame; 'these comfortable words
renewed the terror of La Motte'", us_03_pdf_page:119} — a specific phrase, its rhetorical
figure, and both source and commentary pages. That is exactly the target granularity, for 176
loci plus 99 more inside the unit JSONs. (5) 32,251 words of unit prose carry hundreds more
page-cited assertions unstructured. What blocks the target: the same deliberate suppression of
verbatim text (us-03-radcliffe.json:110 'NOT to be quoted at length per content-filter
discipline'; :234 'NO ≥25-word verbatim from primary text per content-filter discipline'), and
the fact that the loci records store a CONTEXT gloss rather than the clause itself. So this
entry is closer to claim-level than any In-Game unit and closer to clause-level than the
granularity headline suggests — the cost of the programme for entries of this shape is far
lower than a from-scratch estimate, because ~275 claim records and ~275 loci records already
exist and only need IDs, a canonical slot, and the clause text restored alongside the gloss.

================================================================================================
VR Pedagogy Secondary (Part III)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINEST UNIT ACTUALLY ENCODED: the article, decomposed one level down into (a) 233 `sections`
records (title + summary + page, avg 6.9/unit) and (b) 233 `readings` records shaped {claim,
type, stance, locus, tag} (avg 6.9/unit). The `readings` array IS a claim-level slot -- it is
the only place in the entry where a proposition is a first-class addressable object with a
page and a provenance tag. It is roughly ONE level below the article and ONE level above
nothing: at ~7 claims per 20-page article it captures the thesis-bearing claims and discards
everything else.\n\nDISTANCE TO THE TARGET. Claim-level: the slot exists but the density is
off by roughly an order of magnitude. Clause-level: entirely absent -- there is no sentence
id, no character offset, no bbox, no quotation span object anywhere in the entry. Loci are
page numbers and page RANGES ('p.461-465', 'p.15-16, 24-25'), never sub-page anchors, so even
the claims that ARE structured cannot be resolved to a sentence in the source.\n\nCRITICALLY:
a large body of claim-level material IS already present, unstructured, inside the prose
analyses, and this materially lowers the cost of the programme. Quantified over the 34 unit
.md files (529,053 bytes, each with an identical 7-section skeleton):\n  - 1,471 page-locus
mentions of the form p.NN / pp.NN\n  - 522 (FE...) tags = the source's own published claim\n
- 548 FAITHFUL-<author> tags = close paraphrase of a specific passage\n  - 207
(P/anticipatory-application) tags = our interpretive link\n  - 394 quoted spans of >=25
characters\nThat is ~1,277 provenance-tagged assertions in prose against 233 in JSON: roughly
5.5x more claim-level content already written than is currently structured, and each prose
assertion already carries the two fields the structured record needs (a locus and a provenance
tag). The extraction problem is therefore closer to a parsing job than an authoring job. Each
unit .md also carries a section-6 'Concordance feed' markdown table (construct row | media-
condition column | locus | note) -- e.g. 14 rows in ped-sec-06 -- which is already a per-claim
tuple in table form.\n\nWHAT WOULD HAVE TO BE ADDED: (1) a sentence/clause id scheme with
character offsets into a canonical extracted text (none exists; extraction metadata is only
{tool: pdftotext, date, ocr_confidence}); (2) sub-page loci -- the corpus already has a
bbox/verbatim capability elsewhere (WRAITH/Marker), and 33 of 34 units are pdftotext-clean per
the manifest's own OCR audit, so this is feasible; (3) promotion of the 1,277 prose assertions
into readings-shaped records; (4) a stable schema for readings (currently 3 item shapes with
type/stance contamination in 11 items and 5 bare strings); (5) a quotation-span object -- the
394 quoted spans currently have no identity and no per-quote page, since a single paragraph-
terminal (FAITHFUL-X, p.461-465) tag frequently covers 4+ distinct verbatim quotations, which
is exactly how the ped-sec-29 'sense of being in the place' fabrication
survived.\n\nSTRUCTURAL VERDICT ON (a) vs (b): the entry is excellent by its own cluster-
template standard -- 34/34 units complete, 0 stubs (smallest unit JSON is 6,756 bytes), 0
dangling ped-sec ids anywhere in 127 files, concept-matrix perfectly consistent with the
ontology, anchor-pointers table perfectly consistent with the edges. But it is STRUCTURALLY
INCAPABLE of the claim/clause-level target as built, for three reasons that no amount of care
at this layer fixes: the locus vocabulary bottoms out at the page range; there is no id space
below ped-sec-NN (the 60 ontology nodes and 10 DA-NN axes are the only other identifiers, and
40/40 debate edges do not even use the DA ids); and the one channel that reaches runtime
carries 60 concept nodes and nothing else -- no claim, no reading, no edge, no quotation has
any runtime existence at all.

================================================================================================
Veri(dis)similitude (Salvo MA)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This entry is the closest thing in the corpus to the user's target and it should be the
template. vds-claims-moves.md already IS a claim-level index: 157 rows, each with a stable
unique id (A-P01..A-C05, B-*, C-*; zero duplicates, verified), a terse statement, a thesis
page or page-range, a tier, a reuse-disposition from a closed vocabulary (keep-verbatim / re-
ground / enrich / supersede / drop), and one or more RODA anchors from a declared enum
(A0-horizon, A1-perception, A2-phantasma, A3-doxa/emotion, A4-action/hexis, A4->A0',
union(R1-R7), recalcitrance, rupture, hinge, VR<->2D, framing) plus a [V:...] volatility flag.
Add vds-terminology.md (29 ids with type/origin/thesis_pp/RODA mapping), vds-interlocutor-
loci.md (22 sources with per-source cited loci at page granularity - the Heidegger BT row
alone lists ~30 discrete page loci - plus an explicit corpus_index_bridge column naming real
sibling directories), and gg-element-matrix.md (54 ids across 6 typed blocks). That is 262
addressable rows over 15,481 words: roughly one structured claim per 59 words. Distance to
target is therefore ONE step, not two: the unit is already the claim, not the chapter. What is
missing is (a) clause-level decomposition inside each claim's evidence - the 'th p' column
gives a page, never a quoted clause or a line locator, so no claim carries its own verbatim;
(b) serialization - every row is a markdown pipe table, so the ids exist but nothing can
dereference them; (c) edges - the anchors and the corpus_index_bridge column are latent typed
relations (claim -> RODA locus, claim -> interlocutor, source -> sibling entry) that are
stored as comma strings and never materialised. Extraction cost is low and mechanical: five
tables, four of them single-dialect. The one genuine absence is that vds-claims-moves.md
indexes the SOURCE thesis's claims, not the entry's own analytical claims - the 3,883 words of
gg-RODA-matrix.md prose carry perhaps 150-200 further assertions with no addressing at all.

================================================================================================
Virtual Learning Environments (King–Salvo)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest structured unit = a FINDING-STRUCTURE, i.e. a whole survey finding (F1-F8) or one lab-
record case, collapsed into a single ~60-word `definition` string. That is one level ABOVE
chapter granularity in information density, and roughly three levels above clause-level.
Nothing in the entry is addressable below the node: no claim IDs, no clause spans, no offsets,
no quote-to-claim links. BUT the claim-level material is already authored, in prose, in
quantity: vle-04 contains 38 individually numbered, individually page-cited, individually
provenance-tagged catalog items (`grep -cE '^[0-9]+\. \*\*\[' → 38`) — that is a claim-level
index in markdown-list clothing and would convert to records almost mechanically. vle-03 holds
34 verbatim student quotations, each already keyed to respondent+item (W25-R095/Q9,
S25-R022/Q6, …) — that is clause-level evidence with a stable locator scheme, already present.
vle-01 holds 8 numbered findings with ~40 embedded statistics. vle-05 holds 6 numbered
drafting loci plus 9 [FE-STUDENT] verbatims. Across the entry: 124 quoted strings of ≥25 chars
and 57 numbered items in 8,115 words. So the cost of reaching claim level here is NOT
authoring — it is schema plus extraction. What would have to be added: (a) a stable per-claim
ID namespace distinct from `vle-NN`; (b) a claim record carrying {text, register (FE/FE-U/FE-
STUDENT/P), locator (respondent/item or PDF page), status (verified/unverified),
supersedes/superseded-by}; (c) exactly the supersession field whose absence causes this
entry's worst defect — the ontology and the units disagree on 7 findings and no field records
which is current.

================================================================================================
Von Uexkull - A Foray into the Worlds of Animals and Humans
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest STRUCTURED unit: a chapter-pair (14 units, each covering 2 book chapters, 9–17 pages).
But the finest AUTHORED unit is already a claim: the 14 phase2 analyses each carry a '## 4.
Main Positions / Claims' markdown table with columns Claim | Evidence type | Strength | Page
ref — 107 rows in total (f0 6, f1 6, f2 5, f3 9, f4 6, f5 7, f6 7, f7 8, m1 8, m2 6, m3 9, m4
9, m5 9, m6 12). Alongside them sit 180 key-concept rows (Concept | German | Definition |
Centrality | First appearance | Related concepts), 67 tension rows, 73 interlocutor rows, 201
German-term rows and 206 machine-readable local edges in fenced JSON. So claim-level material
EXISTS and is even column-typed with evidence-type and strength grading — a richer claim
schema than Wendt's {text, locus}. It is trapped in markdown tables that nothing parses, and
the corresponding uex-*.json 'unit records' are 800-byte scaffolds with none of it. Distance
to claim level: one extraction pass over 14 files with a stable table schema (Section 3, 4, 5,
7, 8 are identically headed in all 14 — the most uniform prose layer in the batch). Distance
to CLAUSE level: full step further; page refs are page-granular ('pp. 49–50'), there are no
sentence spans or character offsets, and quotations appear inline in the Hierarchical Outline
bullets without any span record. Cost implication: for Uexküll the claim layer is a parse job
over a genuinely regular format, not an authoring job.

================================================================================================
Wendt - Design for Dasein
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Finest encoded unit: a CLAIM. This is the only entry in the batch that already encodes claim-
level structure machine-readably — 139 claim objects across 7 unit JSONs, each {text, locus}
(48 also carry an id), plus 143 key_terms with loci, 105 interlocutors, 68 design_principles
with loci, and 21 ro_hooks. Distance to the claim-level target is therefore ONE step, not two:
the claims exist and are individually page-anchored; what is missing is (a) globally unique
IDs (91/139 have none, 20 collide), (b) linkage from claim → node and claim → edge (the 307
edges and the 139 claims share no key), (c) a uniform schema (8 unit JSONs, 8 shapes), and (d)
any reader. Distance to CLAUSE level is a full step further: nothing in the entry stores a
sentence span, a character offset, or a quote span; the 38 anchor_phrases in dfd-01/dfd-05 are
the closest thing and they are bare strings with no offsets. Additional claim-level material
is ALREADY present but unstructured in the unit .md analyses (~34,100 words of prose that
restate and expand each claim with fuller loci, e.g. dfd-01-...md:114 gives the Bonsiepe claim
with the correct quotation and a page range where the JSON gives a single page). Cost
implication: for Wendt the claim layer is a harvest-and-rekey job, not an authoring job.

